  "metaDescription": "New Delhi faces severe air quality levels following Diwali celebrations as monitoring stations encounter data gaps and political debate intensifies.",
  "aiSummaryBullets": [
    "Air quality in New Delhi reached the severe category following the use of firecrackers during the recent Deepavali festival.",
    "Multiple government air quality monitoring stations in the capital region experienced data outages during the peak pollution period.",
    "Public and political discourse has intensified, with debates emerging over pollution control strategies and the reliability of monitoring systems."
  ],
  "aiContextParagraph": "New Delhi, a metropolis of over 30 million residents, frequently experiences degraded air quality during the autumn and winter months, typically starting in late October. The recent Diwali celebrations saw a significant increase in smog levels, pushing air quality readings into the severe category. Amidst the deteriorating conditions, concerns have been raised regarding the loss of data from dozens of government monitoring stations that went offline during the period of peak pollution. The situation has triggered public debate, with some political figures suggesting unconventional methods to mitigate pollution or dismissing environmental concerns regarding firecracker usage. These developments highlight the ongoing challenges in managing air quality in India's capital, where seasonal meteorological factors often combine with human activities to create hazardous breathing conditions. The lack of continuous, transparent data from monitoring infrastructure has further complicated the assessment of the air quality crisis.",
